{"question_id": "afdc33df", "question_type": "single-session-preference", "response": "Based on what I know about your kitchen, here are some targeted tips:\n\n**Garbage disposal** — Keep up the routine I recommended: pour 1/2 cup baking soda followed by 1 cup vinegar, let it sit 1–2 hours, then rinse with hot water. Doing this every couple of weeks prevents odor buildup (which you were worried about when deciding whether to replace it).\n\n**Countertops** — You bought a utensil holder specifically to keep things clutter-free, so put the organization plan into action: purge duplicates, categorize what's left, assign a home for each group, use dividers, and store frequently used items front and center. Clearing the counters daily is the single biggest mess-killer.\n\n**Granite near the sink** — You noticed scratches there, so be gentle with that spot. Avoid abrasive scrubbers, and if the scratches bother you, the baking soda paste / stone soap / mineral oil / repair kit options I suggested are still on the table (pro refinishing or epoxy filler for deep ones).\n\n**Faucet leak** — That slight leak you mentioned can cause water spots, mineral rings, and even cabinet damage over time, which reads as \"mess.\" It's worth checking the aerator, O-rings, gaskets, valve seats, and handle for wear, cracks, or mineral buildup — or replacing those small parts outright.\n\nOne thing I don't have in memory: any general daily/weekly cleaning schedule for you, so I can't speak to your habits beyond these specific items. If you want, tell me your current routine and I can help spot the gaps.", "n_records": 15, "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 2395, "completion_tokens": 1482, "calls": 2}, "selected": ["self|garbage_disposal_issue", "assistant|garbage_disposal_cleaning_method", "assistant|garbage_disposal_replacement_signs", "self|utensil_holder_purchase", "assistant|utensil_holder_organization_tips", "self|granite_countertop_scratches", "assistant|granite_scratch_repair_methods", "self|ki